    {
      "version": "0.11.0-draft",
      "changes": [
        "fundamentals_screen.modifiers.deceleration_flag.penalty_formula: CORRECTED wording — the formula field literally read 'penalty = -0.15 * min(...)', the same negative-value convention that, if substituted directly into the composite's 'core_sum + peg_modifier - deceleration_penalty' term, produces a double-negative that INCREASES a decelerating stock's score. This was a real trap in the spec's own wording, not just an implementation risk someone might introduce. Reworded to state explicitly that the formula returns a positive magnitude in [0, 0.15], which the composite formula subtracts once."
      ],
      "source": "Claude Code build question, caught while implementing compute_deceleration_penalty() against the literal JSON wording"
    },
    {
      "version": "0.12.0-draft",
      "changes": [
        "relative_strength_computation: RESOLVED the dropna(how='any') vs na_option='keep' discrepancy flagged as an open risk in v0.6. Corrected compute_rs_percentile() (trend_template_test.py) to na_option='keep' -- the production-appropriate behavior at ~224-ticker scale, since dropna(how=\"any\") would blank an entire date for every ticker if even one ticker lacked data that day, a near-certain occurrence somewhere in a real universe on any given day. Proven behavior-preserving for the existing 5-ticker test via a controlled before/after comparison (same fetched data, seconds apart): results byte-identical, since all 5 tickers already had complete, gap-free data.",
        "trend_template_full_universe.py: refactored to import and call the same compute_rs_percentile() function instead of maintaining its own separate inline copy of the formula -- eliminates the discrepancy at its root rather than leaving two implementations that could drift apart again. Reran against its cached 196-ticker dataset post-refactor: identical result to the previously-recorded run (128/196 tickers, 8264 ticker-days), confirming the refactor changed nothing.",
        "test_trend_template_test_B.py: a latent bug was surfaced by the compute_rs_percentile() fix -- the test's own rank-order check assumed the function's output row count already meant 'evaluable days', true only under the old dropna(how='any') behavior (which pre-filtered non-evaluable rows). Under na_option='keep' the function returns all rows including NaN ones, and the test's unfiltered iteration flagged 252 all-NaN warmup rows as fake 'rank order violations'. Fixed by explicitly filtering to genuinely evaluable rows before checking; documented in the file's own docstring per this project's correction-transparency standard."
      ],
      "source": "Claude Code investigation, user-directed (\"tt_9 inner-join vs outer-join discrepancy\" chosen explicitly over other open gaps)"
    },
    {
      "version": "0.13.0-draft",
      "changes": [
        "market_regime_gate: FIRST REVIEW of this block since the project's original seed files -- never touched until now. Found and resolved 6 real gaps against ch09-notes.md/manifesto.md Part 1.",
        "gate_logic: CORRECTED from 'ANY condition true' (OR) to 'ALL conditions true' (AND) -- an OR-gate contradicted the book's high-conviction framing (>90% of superperformance moves began only after the market had already turned) and was inconsistent with hard_filter's own AND-gate precedent for 'one concept expressed multiple ways'",
        "leader_pullback_shallow: added a duration requirement (<=10 trading days) alongside the existing depth requirement (<=10%) -- the book describes this signal as 'brief AND shallow'; the original spec only checked depth",
        "leader_pullback_shallow: RESOLVED 'top-decile-performing stocks' to reuse tt_9's RS percentile (>=90) instead of an undefined separate ranking",
        "new_high_low_ratio_favorable: standardized the trend window from an unexplained 20 trading days to 21, matching the project's existing ~1-month convention (tt_4's SLOPE_MIN_DURATION)",
        "leader_divergence: NEW condition added -- ch09-notes.md's third mechanical regime signal ('leading stocks making higher lows while the general index still makes lower lows') was completely missing from the original 2-condition spec. Operationalized as a breadth-based proxy (% of leader-basket stocks vs. % of full universe showing a rolling higher low), reusing the same 'compare a stat to itself N days earlier' pattern already proven for tt_4. The 15-percentage-point threshold is an invented calibration number, not book-derived -- flagged for separate sign-off from the other fixes in this entry.",
        "leadership_rotation_note: added as an explicit implementation note (not a scoreable condition) -- manifesto.md Part 1 explicitly warns fewer than 25% of one cycle's leaders lead the next; the leader basket must be recomputed fresh each cycle, never cached"
      ],
      "source": "chat review of market_regime_gate against ch09-notes.md and manifesto.md Part 1 -- first review of this block in the project's history"
    },
    {
      "version": "0.14.0-draft",
      "changes": [
        "market_regime_gate.leader_divergence: REPLACED the invented 15-percentage-point magnitude-gap threshold (flagged as unresolved in v0.13) with a directional formulation matching the book's exact two-clause wording: leader_condition (>50% of leader basket shows a higher low) AND universe_condition (<50% of universe shows a higher low). Not a smaller version of the old threshold -- a different kind of check entirely, since the book's sentence ('leading stocks making higher lows WHILE the general index is still making lower lows') describes two simultaneous directional states, not a magnitude gap between two rates. Web research (general market-breadth literature, not EGX-specific, not authoritative) supported this direction too: real breadth-divergence signals are typically directional or use a natural 50% majority pivot, not an invented percentage-point gap -- corroborating rather than driving the fix, since the book's own wording was already sufficient grounds for it.",
        "gate_logic unchanged (still ALL 3 conditions, AND-gate, per v0.13) -- this revision only resolves HOW leader_divergence itself is computed, not whether it's required"
      ],
